Understanding Post-Build Actions in Jenkins
In Jenkins, a build is the process of compiling, testing, and packaging code. But what happens after the build completes? That’s where post-build actions come in.
Post-build actions are automated tasks that Jenkins executes once a build finishes. They help teams stay informed and streamline workflows. Common examples include:
Notifications: Sending emails or messages to developers about build success/failure.
Archiving artifacts: Storing compiled files (like JAR/WAR) for deployment.
Publishing reports: Sharing test results or coverage reports.
Triggering downstream jobs: Starting another pipeline or deployment process.
This ensures that the build process doesn’t stop at compilation—it extends into communication, reporting, and automation.

📧 Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP) in Jenkins
One of the most widely used post-build actions is email notification. Jenkins uses SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol) to send these emails.
SMTP basics:
It’s the standard protocol for sending emails across the internet.
Works by transferring messages from a sender’s mail client/server to the recipient’s mail server.
Typically uses servers like Gmail (smtp.gmail.com) or corporate mail servers.
How Jenkins uses SMTP:
Configure SMTP server details in Jenkins (host, port, credentials).
Define recipients and message content in the job’s post-build actions.
When the build completes, Jenkins sends an email via the SMTP server.

📝 Example Pipeline Script
Here’s a pipeline script that demonstrates post-build actions with SMTP email notifications:

For Success, failure, Aborted:

pipeline {
  agent any

  stages {
    stage('Code') {
      steps {
        echo 'this is code stage'
      }
    }
    stage('Build') {
      steps {
        echo 'this is build stage'
      }
    }
    stage('Test') {
      steps {
        echo 'this is Test stage'
      }
    }
    stage('Deploy') {
      input {
        message "can i deploy?"
      }
      steps {
        echo 'this is Deploy stage'
      }
    }
  }
  post {
    success {
      mail to: 'sample@gmail.com',
      subject: "Build Status: ${currentBuild.fullDisplayName}",
      body: "The build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} finished with status: ${currentBuild.currentResult}. View logs at: ${env.BUILD_URL}"
    }
    failure {
      mail to: 'sample@gmail.com',
      subject: "Build Status: ${currentBuild.fullDisplayName}",
      body: "The build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} finished with status: ${currentBuild.currentResult}. View logs at: ${env.BUILD_URL}"
    }
    aborted {
      mail to: 'sample@gmail.com',
      subject: "Build Status: ${currentBuild.fullDisplayName}",
      body: "The build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} finished with status: ${currentBuild.currentResult}. View logs at: ${env.BUILD_URL}"
    }
  }
}

By using "always" also it will send the mails for success, failure, aborted cases instead of writing individually for three cases
